  "notes": "The only octagonal rooms in the corpus, and they are in the tower: 'The octagonal Breakfast Parlour and Bed rooms above in the Tower have windows commanding views in several directions; and the Bay windows in the other apartments are similarly advantageous.' Both are seventeen feet across, recorded as 17 by 17 because a diameter is what the plate prints and an octagon has no long side. Planned on two corridors crossing, with a conservatory in the angle between them. Walls of limestone faced with hammered stones in irregular courses pointed with blue mortar, quoins of Caen or Bath, grey slates, the cresting to the tower of lead and the vanes of copper. 3500 pounds.",
